Decode data that follows padding in the last piece of input.
read_base64() decodes in pieces, and decode_base64() and
decode_base32() stop after a padded block, leaving the rest of the
piece to be decoded next time round. In the last piece, at end of input
or the stop character, read_base64() instead took any undecoded
characters to be a partial block and failed with "truncated input". So
"YQ==YQ==" followed by a newline decoded to "aa", but without the
newline it printed "a" and failed, and so did "YQ==" followed by any
character the decoder ignores. The patch keeps decoding the last piece,
without reading more input, until decoding makes no progress, and only
then reports truncated input.

---
 libbb/uuencode.c |    9 ++++++---
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/libbb/uuencode.c
+++ b/libbb/uuencode.c
@@ -236,7 +236,7 @@
 		char *out_tail;
 		const char *in_tail;
 
-		while (in_count < BUFFER_SIZE) {
+		while (!term_seen && in_count < BUFFER_SIZE) {
 			int ch = fgetc(src_stream);
 			if (ch == exit_char) {
 				if (in_count == 0)
@@ -276,8 +276,11 @@
 			/* Did we consume ALL characters? */
 			if (*in_tail == '\0')
 				return;
-			/* No */
-			bb_simple_error_msg_and_die("truncated input");
+			/* No. Unless decoding stopped after a padded block
+			 * ("YQ==YQ=="), the rest is a partial block.
+			 */
+			if (in_tail == buf)
+				bb_simple_error_msg_and_die("truncated input");
 		}
 
 		/* It was partial decode */
